    "q-38600": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38600",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the total charge $Q$ of the upper half of the elliptical disk $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4$ with charge density function $\\rho $ given by $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{y}{1+x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The total charge $Q$ of an upper half-disk $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y\\ge 0}$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{y}{1+x}$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\,⟹\\,y\\le 2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117153/assets/a6f503900a79b3d3c9489f20d6e3bbc46fd848b628d5cf43cca99c14279b542f.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the ellipse with the line $y=0.$ So, the $x$-limits are $-1\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117153/assets/91abb4000ba8b04d5f7125d20f05dea76f985503b859203a0d63ba7d5c89e37d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\frac{y}{1+x}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x}[{\\int }_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}y\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+x}(\\frac{4(1-{x}^{2})}{2}-0)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{2(1-x)(1+x)}{1+x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2(1-x)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2x)\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2x)\\,dx & ={[2x-{x}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =((2-1)-(-2-1)) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total charge of the upper-half elliptical disk is $Q=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
