    "q-94400": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94400",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the total mass of the region $D$ enclosed between the curves $y=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $x=e,$ above the line $y=1$, with mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The mass of the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{xy}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $y=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny=1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y-1}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117153/assets/d55a7318d1d7259c6cb644e05a234fcb2adfbbf7799371c8e11ece4fb8a7150a.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II region.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y-1} & =e \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-limits are $1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117153/assets/2afb5f229d56f8b4777ecfe8c1e6b6ba4b414b909cfb0f509cee3c89dac86a84.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type II region: \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,{e}^{y-1}\\le x\\le e,\\,1\\le y\\le 2}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{{e}^{y-1}}^{e}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{y-1})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}(1-(y-1))\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{y}-1)\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{y}-1)\\text{d}y & =[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the given region is $M=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
