    "q-94722": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94722",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117153/assets/b984f3689ac5191b10a1305408c4888ddbddd4df600060b8d0a84a8e1559fbe1.png)\n\nAn object occupies the region $D$ in the fourth quadrant enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$, as shown above. The mass density function $\\lambda $ of the object is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$ Find the mass of the object.",
      "worked_solution": "The mass of an object which occupies the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will convert this to polar coordinates using the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion between the disks of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at the origin. \n\nThe following equations connect cartesian and polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ we obtain $r=1.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ The entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\theta =0,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117153/assets/f2dc1c7b6e558e0f645ad3b6c3d04182554a5778dd7490103bed6ac2e928bcf3.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nD={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{r}{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡r]_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\theta ]_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the object is $M=\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
