      "kp_id": "e-8195"
    },
    "q-93962": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93962",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find the mass of a cardboard object which has the form of a quarter-disk, in the second quadrant, of radius $4$ centered at the origin and mass density function $\\lambda $ given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The mass of a quarter-disk $D$ of radius $4$ centered at the origin with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }\\lambda (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14117153/assets/795390e471ee5e96892dfc979f96b803d4a9a4c267410a3afc60a6672f71e63d.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{4}2\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [2r]_{0}^{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (8-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =8(-(-1)+0) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total mass of the quarter-disk is $M=8.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
